The reflection coefficient measured at the coupling antenna/waveguide is 
\begin{equation}
S_{11}=\frac{Z(\omega)-Z_0}{Z(\omega)+Z_0}
=\frac{\beta - 1 - j Q_0 \delta}{\beta + 1 + j Q_0 \delta}=
\frac{\beta^2-1-(Q_0 \delta)^2}{(\beta+1)^2+(Q_0 \delta)^2}-
      j\frac{2 \beta Q_0 \delta}{(\beta+1)^2+(Q_0 \delta)^2}.
%\quad\mbox{with}\quad
%\quad \delta=\frac{f}{f_0}-\frac{f_0}{f}
%\mbox{Maximum Electric Field} (\mbox{MV/m})= ??? \ P_{RF} (\mbox{MW})
\label{S11}
\end{equation}
%with $\delta=f/f_0-f_0/f$. 
The amplitude of the reflection coefficient is
\begin{equation}
\left|S_{11}\right|=\sqrt{\frac{(\beta-1)^2+(Q_0 \delta)^2}{(\beta+1)^2+(Q_0 \delta)^2}}.
\label{absS11}
\end{equation}
From the amplitude alone, one can not distinguish under-coupled from over-coupled resonance
since the transformation $\beta \to 1/\beta$ and $Q_0 \to Q_0/\beta$ results in the same 
$\left| S_{11} \right|$. Therefore the phase (or the Smith charth plot) must be checked; the
phase reads:
\begin{equation}
\angle S_{11}=
\arctan{\left(\frac{Q_0 \delta}{1-\beta}\right)}-\arctan{\left(\frac{Q_0 \delta}{1+\beta} \right)}
=-\arctan{\left(\frac{2\beta Q_0 \delta}{\beta^2-1-(Q_0 \delta)^2}\right)},
\label{angleS11}
\end{equation}
where the first relation prevents from fictious phase jumps. Around the resonace frequency,
$\angle S_{11}$ can be approximated as
\begin{equation}
\angle S_{11}\approx
\frac{2 \beta}{1-\beta^2} Q_0 \delta = \frac{2 \beta}{1-\beta} Q_L \delta
\quad\mbox{if}\quad
\left|\frac{Q_0\delta}{1-\beta}\right|\ll 1.
\label{angleS11approx}
\end{equation}

